<section class="section" data-section="VII · How We Create Impact" data-break>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal">VII · Our Investment Approach — Growing Resilience</p>
    <p class="lede reveal">Investment in regenerative agriculture correlates strongly with greater resilience and profitability at the farm level.</p>
    <hr class="rule reveal" />
    <p class="reveal">This works through three interconnected mechanisms.</p>

    <p class="h-minor reveal">01 · Lower volatility</p>
    <p class="reveal">Conventional agriculture is highly exposed to a range of risks beyond the farm gate: fluctuating fertiliser prices, unstable energy prices, and drawn-out agrochemical supply chains — all of which are vulnerable to geopolitical shocks. By reducing dependence on synthetic inputs and producing more on-farm — through solar energy, cover crops, and organic alternatives — we aim to ensure that our farms are significantly less exposed to these pressures. Farms producing traceable, certified regenerative outcomes can also target long-term offtake agreements, providing further insulation from commodity market volatility.</p>

    <p class="h-minor reveal">02 · Operational resilience</p>
    <p class="reveal">Healthier soils are more productive soils — and more stable ones. Research shows that regeneratively managed farms maintain around 95 per cent of yields during drought years, compared to around 33 per cent for conventionally managed equivalents.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-16" href="#fn-16">16</a></sup> Each additional 1 per cent of soil organic matter increases water retention by 187 m³ per hectare<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-17" href="#fn-17">17</a></sup> — a material advantage in Mediterranean climates increasingly defined by summer water stress.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Beyond water, healthier soils support healthier plants with greater resistance to disease<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-18" href="#fn-18">18</a></sup>, while on-farm biodiversity — including biological pest control and diverse pollinator populations — provides a further layer of production insurance.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-19" href="#fn-19">19</a></sup></p>

    <p class="h-minor reveal">03 · Higher profitability</p>
    <p class="reveal">Fewer inputs and more stable yields translate directly into stronger margins. This is backed up by a growing evidence base across multiple geographies and crop types: for example, research from UC Davis found that super-high-density olive orchards can maintain yields and oil quality with 25–50 per cent less synthetic nitrogen fertiliser than conventional recommendations,<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-20" href="#fn-20">20</a></sup> while maintaining cover crops in olive orchards has been shown to increase the abundance of natural pest predators, helping to reduce reliance on synthetic pesticides.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-21" href="#fn-21">21</a></sup> Research into regenerative almond production in California shows profitability approximately twice that of conventional orchards.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-22" href="#fn-22">22</a></sup> A Boston Consulting Group analysis found that regenerative practices can increase farm profitability by up to 120 per cent compared to conventional approaches.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-23" href="#fn-23">23</a></sup></p>
  </div>
</section>

