<section class="section" data-section="VII · How We Create Impact" data-break>
  <div class="wide">
    <div class="measure-cols reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow" style="column-span:all">VII · How We Measure Our Impact</p>
      <p>In order to track the impact created through the fund's investments, Pela Terra Farmland adheres to an impact measurement strategy that is built on three major elements:</p>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li>Data collection and expert assessments for each property.</li>
        <li>Reporting frameworks, at the fund level and the farm level.</li>
        <li>Third-party certification programmes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The following section looks at each of these in more detail.</p>

      <p class="h-minor">Data collection and expert assessments for each property</p>
      <p>We collect data on the impact of our work in several ways:</p>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li>In partnership with our farm operators, we collect data on all the inputs we make to the farms controlled by the fund — for example, fuel and energy consumption, fertiliser, herbicide and pesticide use, quantity and type of trees planted, number of employees, type of contract, etc.</li>
        <li>Increasingly, we use AI-enabled software on our farms to help us understand and track our inputs at a granular level.</li>
        <li>To help us better understand outcomes, we also work with external experts who perform detailed assessments of each farm and collect and analyse data on the ground — for example, soil samples to determine soil health and biodiversity, water quality analyses, and habitat surveys to determine what species are present.</li>
        <li>We use drone and satellite imagery to give us a ‘top down’ picture of the properties, helping to identify areas where there are potential issues or areas of particular ecological interest.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="h-minor">Reporting frameworks</p>
      <p>To help us decide which indicators are most important and help us present, analyse and compare the data we collect, we use well-respected, globally recognised frameworks. The two primary frameworks we align with at the fund level are the UN Sustainable Development Goals and the Global Impact Investing Network's IRIS+ framework.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" data-section="VII · How We Create Impact" data-break>
  <div class="wide">
    <div class="measure-cols reveal">
      <p>IRIS+ was developed by the Global Impact Investing Network to be the generally accepted system for impact investors to measure, manage, and optimise their impact. It offers a thematic taxonomy based on generally accepted impact categories and themes and core metrics sets setting out the most useful indicators in each impact category.</p>
      <p>IRIS+ is directly aligned to the UN Sustainable Development Goals (including both the top-level Goals and the targets feeding into them) and over 50 other commonly used metrics frameworks, standards and platforms.</p>
      <p>Using IRIS+ and aligning to the UN Sustainable Development Goals allows us to benchmark the depth and quality of our impact in relation to global standards and link our work to progress towards the outcomes that the UN has decided are most relevant for the sustainable future of the world.</p>
      <p>Starting from this year, Pela Terra Farmland is partnering with Terra Madre to enrol our farms in their Agro Eco Assessment MRV programme, a joint venture with Climate Farmers.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-24" href="#fn-24">24</a></sup></p>
      <p>This provides a comprehensive farm-level assessment across five interdependent impact areas — soil health, biodiversity, water cycle, profitability, and social benefits — evaluated using field data, laboratory analysis, farmer surveys, and scientific literature. Scored on a standardised 1–5 scale in each category, it establishes a quantitative baseline against which each farm's regenerative progress will be tracked over time — while also allowing benchmarking against other farms, regenerative and conventional, in the same region.</p>
      <p>Herdade de Maria Pires &amp; Soberanas de Cima is the first of the fund's properties to enrol in the scheme, and the initial baseline MRV assessment is available later in this report.</p>

      <p class="h-minor">Third-party certification programmes</p>
      <p>Where relevant, we work with partner organisations who provide certifications for processes, properties and produce. These programmes help us to better measure and benchmark the impact we're creating, and also ensure that we're implementing processes that are in line with global standards and best practice.</p>
      <p>Both farms are certified for Integrated Production. Units of the fund are also exploring the possibility of applying for the following certifications, pending further analysis of the ROI of each:</p>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li>Certification through the Pollinator Partnership's Bee Friendly Farming Programme.</li>
        <li>Setting climate and nature targets through the Science-Based Targets Initiative.</li>
        <li>Forest Stewardship Council (FSC) certification for cork forest (montado) areas.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
